1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,440
Green slime on a water tank.

2
00:00:02,440 --> 00:00:04,470
Brown seaweed on the seashore.

3
00:00:04,470 --> 00:00:06,262
A wobbly jelly in a cup.

4
00:00:06,662 --> 00:00:08,215
They look nothing alike.

5
00:00:08,215 --> 00:00:10,155
But all three come from algae.

6
00:00:10,605 --> 00:00:14,935
Diya saw the green slime on the wall
of her water tank and asked: is this

7
00:00:14,935 --> 00:00:15,963
slime a plant?

8
00:00:16,313 --> 00:00:19,367
Algae are the simplest
members of the plant kingdom.

9
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:22,700
And they come in three
colours: green, brown and red.

10
00:00:23,200 --> 00:00:27,388
By the end, you will know what makes
an alga, how algae reproduce, and

11
00:00:27,388 --> 00:00:29,549
how to tell the three groups apart.

12
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:31,619
So what is an alga?

13
00:00:31,969 --> 00:00:35,921
Algae have chlorophyll, so they
make their own food from sunlight.

14
00:00:35,921 --> 00:00:37,609
That is called autotrophic.

15
00:00:38,059 --> 00:00:39,778
Their body is a thallus.

16
00:00:39,778 --> 00:00:43,884
That means it is not divided
into a real root, stem and leaf.

17
00:00:44,334 --> 00:00:46,762
Most live in water, fresh or salty.

18
00:00:46,762 --> 00:00:49,538
Some live on moist stones, soil and wood.

19
00:00:49,538 --> 00:00:53,374
Some even live with fungi, or
on the fur of a sloth bear.

20
00:00:54,024 --> 00:00:57,137
Now let's zoom into the
slime, one level at a time.

21
00:00:57,487 --> 00:01:00,635
From far away, it is just
a green coat on the wall.

22
00:01:01,135 --> 00:01:02,035
Zoom in.

23
00:01:01,933 --> 00:01:05,512
Slime like this is often
made of thin green threads.

24
00:01:05,512 --> 00:01:09,838
Each thread is a chain of cells,
like a filament of Spirogyra.

25
00:01:10,338 --> 00:01:11,238
Zoom again.

26
00:01:11,199 --> 00:01:15,680
One cell, with its cell wall, a nucleus
in the middle, and a chloroplast

27
00:01:15,680 --> 00:01:17,167
twisted like a ribbon.

28
00:01:17,667 --> 00:01:19,499
Zoom into the chloroplast.

29
00:01:19,499 --> 00:01:22,600
Small round bodies called
pyrenoids sit on it.

30
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:25,056
They store protein, besides starch.

31
00:01:25,706 --> 00:01:28,457
Algae also come in very different sizes.

32
00:01:28,807 --> 00:01:32,304
Volvox is a colony, a
hollow ball of many cells.

33
00:01:32,754 --> 00:01:35,269
Ulothrix and Spirogyra are filaments.

34
00:01:35,269 --> 00:01:40,089
And kelps in the sea grow into huge
bodies, as long as a tall tree.

35
00:01:40,739 --> 00:01:42,826
How do algae make more algae?

36
00:01:42,826 --> 00:01:43,758
Three ways.

37
00:01:44,108 --> 00:01:45,678
Way one, vegetative.

38
00:01:45,678 --> 00:01:50,273
The filament simply breaks into
pieces, and each piece grows into a new

39
00:01:50,273 --> 00:01:51,173
alga.

40
00:01:50,847 --> 00:01:52,416
This is fragmentation.

41
00:01:53,166 --> 00:01:54,562
Way two, asexual.

42
00:01:54,562 --> 00:01:56,171
The alga makes spores.

43
00:01:56,171 --> 00:01:58,245
The most common are zoospores.

44
00:01:58,245 --> 00:02:01,725
They have flagella, tiny
whips, so they can swim.

45
00:02:02,475 --> 00:02:04,056
Way three, sexual.

46
00:02:04,056 --> 00:02:05,470
Two gametes fuse.

47
00:02:06,220 --> 00:02:09,784
If both gametes are the
same size, it is isogamous.

48
00:02:09,784 --> 00:02:14,641
They may have flagella, as in
Chlamydomonas, or none, as in Spirogyra.

49
00:02:15,391 --> 00:02:20,359
If one gamete is a large egg that
cannot move, and the other is small and

50
00:02:20,359 --> 00:02:23,793
swims, it is oogamous,
as in Volvox and Fucus.

51
00:02:24,543 --> 00:02:25,523
Your turn.

52
00:02:25,523 --> 00:02:29,299
A large still egg and a
tiny swimming gamete fuse.

53
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:31,024
Isogamous or oogamous?

54
00:02:31,024 --> 00:02:32,466
Pause and decide.

55
00:02:35,666 --> 00:02:36,566
Oogamous!

56
00:02:36,377 --> 00:02:40,863
The two gametes are very different:
a big egg that stays, and a small one

57
00:02:40,863 --> 00:02:42,116
that swims to it.

58
00:02:42,766 --> 00:02:43,778
Remember this.

59
00:02:43,778 --> 00:02:48,009
Algae are sorted into three classes
by their pigments, their stored food

60
00:02:48,009 --> 00:02:49,419
and their cell wall.

61
00:02:50,019 --> 00:02:52,489
Let's build the table, one row at a time.

62
00:02:52,839 --> 00:02:55,044
Chlorophyceae, the green algae.

63
00:02:55,044 --> 00:02:56,580
Chlorophyll a and b.

64
00:02:56,580 --> 00:02:57,950
They store starch.

65
00:02:57,950 --> 00:02:59,640
Cell wall of cellulose.

66
00:02:59,640 --> 00:03:02,402
Two to eight equal flagella at the tip.

67
00:03:02,852 --> 00:03:05,047
Phaeophyceae, the brown algae.

68
00:03:05,047 --> 00:03:09,194
Chlorophyll a and c, and a brown
pigment called fucoxanthin.

69
00:03:09,194 --> 00:03:11,557
They store mannitol and laminarin.

70
00:03:11,557 --> 00:03:13,930
The wall has cellulose and algin.

71
00:03:13,930 --> 00:03:16,303
Two unequal flagella on the side.

72
00:03:16,753 --> 00:03:18,901
Rhodophyceae, the red algae.

73
00:03:18,901 --> 00:03:23,163
Chlorophyll a and d, and a red
pigment called phycoerythrin.

74
00:03:23,163 --> 00:03:25,203
They store floridean starch.

75
00:03:25,203 --> 00:03:26,757
No flagella at all.

76
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:29,857
Most brown and red algae live in the sea.

77
00:03:29,857 --> 00:03:33,991
Red algae live near the surface, and
even deep down where little light

78
00:03:33,991 --> 00:03:34,891
reaches.

79
00:03:35,303 --> 00:03:36,820
Algae matter to us too.

80
00:03:37,170 --> 00:03:41,808
Algae do at least half of all the
carbon dioxide fixing on Earth.

81
00:03:41,808 --> 00:03:45,524
Porphyra, Laminaria and
Sargassum are eaten as food.

82
00:03:45,524 --> 00:03:50,367
Agar, from the red algae Gelidium
and Gracilaria, sets jellies and ice

83
00:03:50,367 --> 00:03:53,033
creams, and grows microbes in labs.

84
00:03:53,683 --> 00:03:55,347
Now, a worked example.

85
00:03:55,347 --> 00:03:57,621
Sort six algae into their classes.

86
00:03:57,971 --> 00:04:00,913
Chlamydomonas and Volvox: green algae.

87
00:04:01,363 --> 00:04:03,767
Fucus and Laminaria: brown algae.

88
00:04:04,217 --> 00:04:06,589
Porphyra and Gelidium: red algae.

89
00:04:06,589 --> 00:04:10,199
The jelly in Diya's cup came
from algae like Gelidium.

90
00:04:10,849 --> 00:04:12,315
Now, the exam trap.

91
00:04:12,665 --> 00:04:16,310
Many students think brown and
red algae have no chlorophyll.

92
00:04:16,710 --> 00:04:17,610
They do.

93
00:04:17,494 --> 00:04:19,997
All three classes have chlorophyll a.

94
00:04:19,997 --> 00:04:23,295
The brown and red pigments
simply hide the green.

95
00:04:23,945 --> 00:04:25,350
Where is this asked?

96
00:04:25,350 --> 00:04:29,850
As one line features from the table:
pigments, stored food, flagella, or

97
00:04:29,850 --> 00:04:30,750
an example.

98
00:04:31,318 --> 00:04:32,536
Exam level now.

99
00:04:32,536 --> 00:04:34,332
Read the four statements.

100
00:04:34,332 --> 00:04:36,025
Which ones are correct?

101
00:04:36,025 --> 00:04:37,295
Pause and solve.

102
00:04:42,695 --> 00:04:43,961
The answer is C.

103
00:04:43,961 --> 00:04:45,620
Statements two and four.

104
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:47,246
One is wrong.

105
00:04:47,246 --> 00:04:50,832
Floridean starch is stored
by red algae, not brown.

106
00:04:51,332 --> 00:04:52,510
Three is wrong.

107
00:04:52,510 --> 00:04:54,876
Red algae have no flagella at all.

108
00:04:55,476 --> 00:04:56,937
Here is your recap.

109
00:04:56,937 --> 00:04:58,439
Screenshot this card.

110
00:05:01,639 --> 00:05:04,476
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

111
00:05:04,876 --> 00:05:05,776
One.

112
00:05:05,382 --> 00:05:07,061
What does thalloid mean?

113
00:05:07,961 --> 00:05:08,861
Two.

114
00:05:08,498 --> 00:05:12,107
What is the difference between
isogamous and oogamous?

115
00:05:13,007 --> 00:05:13,907
Three.

116
00:05:13,648 --> 00:05:15,540
Which algae give us agar?

117
00:05:16,640 --> 00:05:21,071
Next time: soft velvet moss on
the compound wall in the monsoon.

118
00:05:21,071 --> 00:05:22,244
Why only then?

119
00:05:22,244 --> 00:05:24,164
We find out in bryophytes.
